Chapter: TerrainModel
  cites Clouds chapter CloudModel
  cites Water chapter OceanModel

 The two techniques worth the name:

   **Triplanar mapping** solves a problem that only exists because a
   heightfield has no texture coordinates. Projecting a pattern straight
   down works until the ground tilts, and on a cliff face a downward
   projection smears into vertical streaks. Triplanar projects the pattern
   three times, once down each axis, and blends the three by how much the
   surface faces that axis. Nothing is ever stretched, because whichever
   projection would stretch is exactly the one whose weight is near zero.

   **Height blending** replaces a linear fade between two materials. A
   lerp between rock and grass gives a soft grey band that looks like
   neither, and it is the single most recognisable tell of a first terrain
   shader. Blending on a per-material HEIGHT instead lets the taller
   material win locally, so grass appears first in the hollows of the rock
   and the boundary interlocks the way a real one does.

Section: Ridged Noise

 Mountains are not made of the same signal as hills. A plain fractal sum is
 symmetric about its mean and gives rolling dunes; folding it about its
 midpoint and squaring puts a CREASE at every ridge line, which is what
 reads as a mountain. Both are summed here, because a landscape that is all
 ridges is as wrong as one that is all dunes.

  t-abs : Real -> Real
  t-abs (x) = if x < 0.0 then 0.0 - x else x

  t-ridge-one : V3, Real -> Real
  t-ridge-one (p) (freq) =
    let n = c-noise (v-scale p freq)
    in let r = 1.0 - t-abs (2.0 * n - 1.0)
    in r * r

  t-ridge-acc : V3, Integer, Real, Real, Real, Real -> Real
  t-ridge-acc (p) (octaves) (freq) (amp) (sum) (norm) =
    if octaves <= 0 then (if norm ~ 0.0 then 0.0 else sum / norm)
    else let s2 = sum + amp * t-ridge-one p freq
    in let n2 = norm + amp
    in t-ridge-acc p (octaves - 1) (freq * 2.0) (amp * 0.5) s2 n2

  t-ridged : V3, Integer -> Real
  t-ridged (p) (octaves) = t-ridge-acc p octaves 1.0 1.0 0.0 0.0

Section: The Height Field

  t-at : Real, Real -> V3
  t-at (x) (z) = V3 { x = x, y = 0.0, z = z }

  t-height : Real, Real -> Real
  t-height (x) (z) =
    let p = t-at (x * terrain-freq) (z * terrain-freq)
    in let ridges = t-ridged p terrain-ridge-octaves
    in let rolling = c-fbm p terrain-roll-octaves
    in let h = ridges * terrain-ridge-weight + rolling * terrain-roll-weight
    in h * terrain-amplitude

  t-normal : Real, Real -> V3
  t-normal (x) (z) =
    let e = terrain-normal-eps
    in let hl = t-height (x - e) z
    in let hr = t-height (x + e) z
    in let hd = t-height x (z - e)
    in let hu = t-height x (z + e)
    in v-norm (V3 { x = hl - hr, y = 2.0 * e, z = hd - hu })

Section: Triplanar Projection

 The sharpness is what makes the technique work rather than merely average.
 At sharpness 1 all three projections contribute almost everywhere and the
 result is a mush of three misaligned copies of the pattern. Raising it
 concentrates the weight on the dominant axis, so a nearly flat surface is
 nearly pure top projection and only genuinely diagonal ground blends.

  t-tri-weights : V3 -> V3
  t-tri-weights (n) =
    let ax = w-powi (t-abs (n.x)) terrain-tri-sharpness
    in let ay = w-powi (t-abs (n.y)) terrain-tri-sharpness
    in let az = w-powi (t-abs (n.z)) terrain-tri-sharpness
    in let s = ax + ay + az
    in if s ~ 0.0 then V3 { x = 0.0, y = 1.0, z = 0.0 }
    else V3 { x = ax / s, y = ay / s, z = az / s }

  t-pattern : Real, Real, Real -> Real
  t-pattern (u) (v) (scale) =
    let p = t-at (u * scale) (v * scale)
    in c-fbm p terrain-detail-octaves

 The projection itself. Note WHICH pair of coordinates each plane uses: the
 x-facing projection is indexed by z and y, and drops x. Getting these
 wrong is silent -- the image still looks textured -- and shows up only as a
 pattern that slides along a cliff as the camera moves.

  t-triplanar : V3, V3, Real -> Real
  t-triplanar (p) (w) (scale) =
    let sx = t-pattern (p.z) (p.y) scale
    in let sy = t-pattern (p.x) (p.z) scale
    in let sz = t-pattern (p.x) (p.y) scale
    in sx * w.x + sy * w.y + sz * w.z

Section: Height-Blended Layers

  Mat = record { col : V3, hgt : Real }

  t-height-blend : Mat, Real, Mat, Real -> V3
  t-height-blend (a) (wa) (b) (wb) =
    let ka = wa + a.hgt * terrain-splat-depth
    in let kb = wb + b.hgt * terrain-splat-depth
    in let m = if ka > kb then ka else kb
    in let d = m - terrain-splat-width
    in let ba = if ka - d > 0.0 then ka - d else 0.0
    in let bb = if kb - d > 0.0 then kb - d else 0.0
    in let s = ba + bb
    in if s ~ 0.0 then a.col
    else let mixed = v-add (v-scale (a.col) ba) (v-scale (b.col) bb)
    in v-scale mixed (1.0 / s)

  t-surface : V3, V3 -> V3
  t-surface (p) (n) =
    let w = t-tri-weights n
    in let detail = t-triplanar p w terrain-detail-scale
    in let coarse = t-triplanar p w terrain-coarse-scale
    in let splat = t-triplanar p w terrain-splat-scale
    in let rock-col = v-lerp terrain-rock-dark terrain-rock-light detail
    in let grass-col = v-lerp terrain-grass-dark terrain-grass-light coarse
    in let rock = Mat { col = rock-col, hgt = splat }
    in let grass = Mat { col = grass-col, hgt = 1.0 - splat }
    in let slope = c-remap (n.y) terrain-slope-lo terrain-slope-hi 0.0 1.0
    in let ground = t-height-blend grass slope rock (1.0 - slope)
    in let alt = c-remap (p.y) terrain-snow-lo terrain-snow-hi 0.0 1.0
    in let snow = Mat { col = terrain-snow-color, hgt = 1.0 - splat }
    in let below = Mat { col = ground, hgt = splat }
    in t-height-blend snow alt below (1.0 - alt)

Section: Shading

  t-shade : V3, V3 -> V3
  t-shade (p) (n) =
    let albedo = t-surface p n
    in let ndl = w-sat (v-dot n (v-norm terrain-sun))
    in let direct = v-scale terrain-sun-color ndl
    in let light = v-add direct (v-scale terrain-sky-color terrain-ambient)
    in V3 {
      x = albedo.x * light.x,
      y = albedo.y * light.y,
      z = albedo.z * light.z
    }

Section: Scene Configuration

  terrain-freq : Real
  terrain-freq = 0.0016

  terrain-amplitude : Real
  terrain-amplitude = 260.0

  terrain-ridge-octaves : Integer
  terrain-ridge-octaves = 5

  terrain-roll-octaves : Integer
  terrain-roll-octaves = 3

  terrain-ridge-weight : Real
  terrain-ridge-weight = 0.72

  terrain-roll-weight : Real
  terrain-roll-weight = 0.28

  terrain-normal-eps : Real
  terrain-normal-eps = 1.5

  terrain-tri-sharpness : Integer
  terrain-tri-sharpness = 4

  terrain-detail-octaves : Integer
  terrain-detail-octaves = 2

  terrain-detail-scale : Real
  terrain-detail-scale = 0.09

  terrain-coarse-scale : Real
  terrain-coarse-scale = 0.021

  terrain-splat-depth : Real
  terrain-splat-depth = 0.85

  terrain-splat-width : Real
  terrain-splat-width = 0.55

 Raising the depth breaks the contour, and then two further things go
 wrong in turn. Too fine a texture ALIASES: at 0.45 the features are about
 two metres, a pixel two kilometres out covers eight, and the boundary
 dissolves into per-pixel speckle. Too much depth OVERRIDES the weight
 entirely, and snow appears in patches on low ground because its height
 beat the altitude that was supposed to decide the matter. The settled
 values keep the feature size above the pixel footprint at the distances
 this scene actually renders, and keep the perturbation comparable to,
 not larger than, the weight it perturbs.

  terrain-splat-scale : Real
  terrain-splat-scale = 0.05

  terrain-slope-lo : Real
  terrain-slope-lo = 0.76

  terrain-slope-hi : Real
  terrain-slope-hi = 0.88

  terrain-snow-lo : Real
  terrain-snow-lo = 178.0

  terrain-snow-hi : Real
  terrain-snow-hi = 208.0

  terrain-rock-dark : V3
  terrain-rock-dark = V3 { x = 0.24, y = 0.22, z = 0.20 }

  terrain-rock-light : V3
  terrain-rock-light = V3 { x = 0.52, y = 0.49, z = 0.45 }

  terrain-grass-dark : V3
  terrain-grass-dark = V3 { x = 0.16, y = 0.26, z = 0.11 }

  terrain-grass-light : V3
  terrain-grass-light = V3 { x = 0.34, y = 0.46, z = 0.20 }

  terrain-snow-color : V3
  terrain-snow-color = V3 { x = 0.92, y = 0.94, z = 0.97 }

  terrain-sun : V3
  terrain-sun = V3 { x = 0.55, y = 0.62, z = 0.56 }

  terrain-sun-color : V3
  terrain-sun-color = V3 { x = 1.06, y = 0.99, z = 0.88 }

  terrain-sky-color : V3
  terrain-sky-color = V3 { x = 0.42, y = 0.55, z = 0.78 }

  terrain-ambient : Real
  terrain-ambient = 0.34

